  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 143 PM CDT SAT JUL 4 2026\n\nAfternoon shower and thunderstorm chances today will remain on\nthe disorganized side, given a lack of upper-level support. In\nturn, thunderstorm chances today will continue to be dictated by\npeak afternoon heating and outflow boundaries. By tomorrow\nhowever, a trough will begin to organize over the Midwest/Great\nLakes region dropping south as the day progresses. Given the\nbetter flow aloft associated with this trough and a remnant MCV, \nrain chances appear to be more organized during the afternoon \nhours on Sunday. The trough should linger across the region into \nMonday, allowing for slightly more organized rain chances here as \nwell, before finally moving off the east coast.\n\nRain chances by the middle of the new workweek should more\nresemble the usual diurnally driven afternoon convection that\nwe're used to seeing in the summer. Closer to the weekend\nhowever, it looks like another weak trough could start to skirt\nthe region, creating more organized rain chances once more. Even\nthough confidence here is low, rain chances will remain\nregardless, just much less organized. Due to these aforementioned\nrain chances, afternoon highs will drop a few degrees as well,\nsettling into the low to mid-90s for much of the workweek. Given\nthese lower temperatures and increased cloud cover, heat index\nvalues will drop as well, allowing us to drop below any kind of\nHeat Advisory criteria.\n\n/44/",
